ont that no  polling i have seen has shown that as  effective  um and there was actually a poll out  yesterday that showed that  president biden um and teachers were  the most trusted uh entities in terms of  determining when school should reopen we  obviously  uh rely on science and rely on our  health experts  and we think that reflects uh where most  of the country want us to be they want  schools to reopen we're also committed  to doing that so there are cdc  guidelines that came out  on friday that provided a  roadmap for schools to open uh  safely the president wants schools to  open uh  five days a week he wants kids to be in  school teachers want kids to be in  school  and he also believes that teachers  should be prioritized as do  as does the vice president so but he  also believes  that there are a range of mitigation  steps or i should say he follows the  guidelines as we all  do and should that were put out by our  health and medical experts  that show there are a range of  mitigation steps vaccinations are one of  the additional steps that the cdc is  recommending  but there are additional steps including  masking smaller class sizes  and he is eager to have his secretary of  education confirmed  so that he can work with school  districts across the country  lead that effort to to  reopen schools and we remain committed  to that  any concern about this issue though  making democrats vulnerable  in 2022 if it's not turned around if  schools don't start rebuilding  here in the coming months well i would  say that the president the vice  president  and this white house don't see reopening  schools  through a political prism we see it as  what's in the interest of teachers of  students of families of parents and we  want to do it safely  and the president is committed to  reopening schools  five days a week as quickly as possible  he is committed to also following  science  and working with school districts to  having his secretary of education work  with school districts  to get that done uh and he is also what  is it within his power  is that we we are working with congress  to get  additional funding which is essential to  many school districts across the country  so that they can follow and take these  mitigation steps recommended by the cdc  including interest masking smaller class  sizes uh that  there are more bus drivers hired that  there are more teachers hired if needed  uh that's within the president's power  something he's focused on every day and  night  so that we can open schools open them  five days a week  uh within 100 days and  that's where focus is we don't see that  through a political prison kids are not  democrats or republicans  and their parents i think this is an  issue that all americans care deeply  about  thanksgiving  our next question will come from the  line of  kayla tausch of cnbc please go ahead  hi kayla hi jen thank you for doing this  we appreciate it  i have two questions one domestic one  foreign the first  is on the economy this week we saw  retail sales and inflation data  that were unexpectedly strong i'm  wondering how the white house is  factoring that data into the size of the  rescue package  and whether 1.9 trillion dollars would  as some critics say  overheat the economy and then my foreign  question  which i'll just go ahead and ask is that  iran has said it will stop allowing snap  inspections  by the iaea beginning on monday if  parties to the nuclear deal don't take  their own steps toward full compliance  does the president see this as  motivation to engage with iran sooner  rather than later  well uh let me take the second question  first  if that makes sense um iran is a long  way from compliance  our focus is on working with our  partners and allies to  to engage and coordinate on a range of  issues including  the future of the iran nuclear deal uh  secretary  blinken has a meeting with his e3  counterparts  later this afternoon and i would expect  the state department would have a  readout from that meeting but certainly  uh our uh our focus is of course on  preventing iran from acquiring a nuclear  reveal  but they are a long way from compliance  and our on our focus is also on working  with our partners and allies  on uh uh our priority i should say kayla  on on the  uh economic question i know a number of  our economic experts including uh  uh secretary uh our secretary of  treasury have answered this question  but i will say that um most economists  will tell  you and most economic data will show  that  we are crawling out of a massive hole  and it's we're crawling out too slowly  and that uh what is essential is to  ensure that we have um  uh we put in uh you know  of course stimulus into our economy uh  to help  uh expedite that and that is part of  what this package will do  by providing direct relief to the  american people by reopening schools  which will have a huge impact on  working mothers and parents across the  country by getting vaccinations in the  arms of the american people  most economic data and studies have  shown that  this package will have a significant  impact on that  and that we still have a long way to go  in our recovery so it is essential at  this point in time  thank you appreciate it sure our next  question will come from the line of  peter alexander of  nbc news please go ahead hi jeff thanks  thanks for hosting us sorry we can't do  it in person you sort of hit on this a  bit but i just want to  sort of drill down a little bit on the  immigration announcement  today and what specifically is the  strategy for this white house to get  this  immigration bill passed right now will  you bring republicans here to engage  them in the process would you do it  through reconciliation  how are you going to get something this  big done sure  all great questions peter i think we're  not quite there uh  because today is the day that the bill  is being proposed uh  obviously senator menendez uh  congresswoman sanchez will be  key leaders uh on the hill to uh  determine  in coordination with us the next steps  forward  but right now uh we are eager to  communicate about what is in the package  why the key components why all of the  components are pivotal pieces of the  package  and in terms of the mechanisms or the  the  um timeline or the mechanics uh you know  we're we're happy to have that  conversation in the weeks ahead but  today  uh we're just it's the bill is just  being officially introduced  thank you our next question  i'm just going to take two more  questions and we'll of course do this  again tomorrow but i just have a  bit of a heart out coming up but go  ahead whoever's next  our next question will come from the  line of matt visser of the washington  post please go ahead  uh hey jim thanks for doing this in an  unusual circumstance um we we know  from earlier that biden spoke with  governors on tuesday  about the weather conditions  um most of his visibility though has  been on  another disaster of covid um can you  highlight a little bit  just how hands-on biden himself  has been with the with the natural  disasters if he's briefed  hourly daily if he has any plans to  visit  any of these states or plans to say  anything  more broadly about it at this time  sure i will say that the president is  kept  abreast of the developments um in texas  and the surrounding states  and receives updates uh every day but  more more than more than once a day  um it is uh certainly a focus um and as  uh and uh ensuring that the people of  texas the people of the surrounding  states have the  resources they need is something that he  raped us  um in in meetings uh frequently and has  over the past couple of days  in terms of whether he will visit you  know i think as you well know matt  one of the factors to consider here is  what the impact is the footprint  right of a presidential trip uh it can  take up resources it can take up  um you know the time and energy of  police and security and so those are  factors that we consider as we determine  when and where he will visit  but he is does receive he is in the  white house today of course that's where  he lives but he is  i should say um you know working today  receiving updates today  i expect he would receive uh several  updates from his national security team  and he is focused on and has directed  his team  to provide him uh ensure he's updated  but also make  rapid decisions and be responsive to the  specific needs of the states as they  come up during this difficult time  thank you okay i'll take uh one more  question and we'll  we'll do this again tomorrow well  different version of this but  our next question and q will come from  the line  of mr james rosen of sinclair broadcast  group  please go ahead thank you jen can hear  me all right  i can hi jane jen thanks for expanding  the call today and it's nice to be back  with you if only by telephone  uh i just wanted to  follow up on the comments of liz to have  the deputy national security adviser  brief us  on the texas grid collapse  you know it's not just the immediate  logistical issues that she's grappling  with  i want to ask about the nexus between  energy policy and national security  as you know republicans in congress and  in the energy sector  have been pointing out that uh according  to data  from the u.s energy information  administration the u.s achieved  uh one of the core benchmarks of energy  independence in 2019  that was the first year since 1957 that  the u.s produced more energy than the  country  consumed and that's one basic definition  of energy independence and  they claim or they argue that the  policies being pursued by the bible  administration will sacrifice those  gains how do you persuade the american  people that u.s  national security can be safeguarded by  this transition to a green economy  which of course has never been fully  developed anywhere relative to to our  scale  well james you know in this white house  we like to  follow the facts and also rely on  experts  as you know and i would say that um  you know officials at the electric  reliability council of texas which  operates the state's power grid  has said that the failures in winds and  solar were the least significant factor  in the blackouts  and there have been a range of reports  that have suggested otherwise  inaccurately but that's not accurate  according to the electoral  liability council of texas as dr  sherwood  randall also conveyed um clearly um  there is a need to assess  uh how we uh protect and and and  support our national infrastructure uh  to ensure it's resilient  uh and sustainable during  storms during any threats to it  and there is of course plenty of time to  do that and it is a priority for this  administration but right now  our focus for focus the president's  focus is on  ensuring that the millions of people or  the many many people across these states  the states that are impacted  are receiving the relief and assistance  they need uh so we will have those  policy discussions  uh but we are focused on the emergency  at hand at this point in time  thank you everybody so much for joining  uh the briefing really appreciate your  flexibility and  getting through the technical details  and i'll look forward to talking with  all of you tomorrow
 

